<SiteLayout title="Module 12: IPv6 Addressing — fred.fish" description="IPv6 expands the address space to 128 bits and uses hexadecimal notation. Learn the address types, compression rules, and methods hosts use to configure themselves." active="cisco">
<section class="source-lesson">
<div class="lesson-nav"><a href="/cisco/learning/semester-1/">← Semester 1 course</a><a href="/cisco/practice/">Adaptive practice ↗</a></div>
<header class="lesson-hero"><Eyebrow index="S1 / 12" label="MODULE 12 / IPV6 ADDRESSING" /><h1>Use the 128-bit space.<br /><span>Read the prefix.</span></h1><p>IPv6 expands the address space to 128 bits and uses hexadecimal notation. Learn the address types, compression rules, and methods hosts use to configure themselves.</p></header>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">01</div><div><h2>Read and abbreviate IPv6 addresses</h2><p>An IPv6 address is 128 bits, written as eight 16-bit hextets in hexadecimal separated by colons. Leading zeros in a hextet may be omitted. One contiguous sequence of all-zero hextets may be compressed with :: once in an address. The prefix length identifies the network portion, commonly /64 on LANs.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-0959 · IMG-0955</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">02</div><div><h2>Recognize IPv6 address types</h2><p>Global unicast addresses are globally routable and come from 2000::/3. Link-local addresses use FE80::/10 and are valid only on the local link. Multicast addresses use FF00::/8. The loopback address is ::1 and the unspecified address is ::. IPv6 does not use broadcast; multicast performs many group-delivery functions.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-0937 · IMG-0970 · IMG-0978 · IMG-0945 · IMG-0935</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">03</div><div><h2>Configure IPv6 dynamically</h2><p>A host can use static configuration or learn network information dynamically. SLAAC uses Router Advertisements to help a host create its own global unicast address when the advertised prefix has the Autonomous flag set. With RA flags M=0/O=0, hosts use SLAAC only; M=0/O=1 means SLAAC plus stateless DHCPv6 for other settings; M=1 indicates stateful DHCPv6 address assignment. A link-local address is required on every IPv6-enabled interface for local communication and Neighbor Discovery.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-0938 · IMG-0965</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">04</div><div><h2>Configure global and link-local addresses</h2><p>An IPv6-enabled interface needs a link-local address as well as any global unicast address used for routed communication. A link-local address can be generated automatically or assigned manually with the link-local keyword. Modified EUI-64 can derive a 64-bit interface ID by inserting FFFE between the MAC halves and flipping the universal/local bit, but modern systems often use stable or temporary privacy identifiers instead. Do not assume an IPv6 interface ID reveals its hardware MAC.</p><pre><code>interface gigabitEthernet 0/0/0
 ipv6 address 2001:db8:acad:1::1/64
 ipv6 address fe80::1 link-local
 no shutdown
show ipv6 interface brief</code></pre><p>Use Duplicate Address Detection (DAD) before an address is used. DAD sends a Neighbor Solicitation for the tentative address; if another node responds, the address must not be assigned.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE TOPICS</span><code>GUA · LLA · EUI-64 · Privacy IDs · DAD</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">05</div><div><h2>Use Neighbor Discovery and IPv6 multicast</h2><p>IPv6 uses ICMPv6 Neighbor Discovery rather than ARP. Neighbor Solicitation (NS) and Neighbor Advertisement (NA) resolve neighbors and support DAD. Router Solicitation (RS) asks routers to send Router Advertisements (RA); Redirect tells a host that a better first hop exists. Common link-local multicast groups include FF02::1 (all nodes) and FF02::2 (all routers). Neighbor Solicitation uses a solicited-node multicast address derived from the target address’s low-order 24 bits. This is more selective than sending every solicitation to all nodes.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE TOPICS</span><code>NS · NA · RS · RA · Redirect · Solicited-node multicast</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">06</div><div><h2>Plan IPv6 /64 subnets from a /48</h2><p>With a /48 global routing prefix and /64 LAN prefixes, the 16 bits between them form the subnet ID. This provides 65,536 possible /64 LAN prefixes. For example, 2001:DB8:ACAD:00A2::/64 uses subnet ID 00A2 within 2001:DB8:ACAD::/48. A hierarchical plan may split those 16 bits into site, sub-site, and subnet fields. Concatenate the allocated bit fields to form the fourth hextet, then append ::/64. Keep the /48-to-/64 boundary clear: the interface identifier begins after the first 64 bits.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE TOPICS</span><code>/48 global prefix · 16-bit subnet ID · /64 interface ID</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">07</div><div><h2>Choose an IPv4/IPv6 coexistence method</h2><p>Dual stack runs IPv4 and IPv6 side by side. Tunneling encapsulates one protocol inside the other to cross an incompatible transit network. Translation, such as NAT64, enables communication between IPv6-only and IPv4-only systems. These methods solve different migration constraints; they are not interchangeable and do not replace native end-to-end IPv6 where it is available.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE TOPICS</span><code>Dual stack · Tunneling · Translation</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">08</div><div><h2>Separate addressing from routing configuration</h2><p>The main objective of this module is IPv6 addressing. Commands such as ipv6 unicast-routing and ipv6 route ::/0 are useful operational extensions: the first enables a router to forward IPv6 unicast packets, and the second configures an IPv6 default static route. Treat these as routing/configuration enrichment rather than a substitute for mastering address types, GUA/LLA configuration, SLAAC/DHCPv6 behavior, multicast, and subnet IDs.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>ENRICHMENT</span><code>ipv6 unicast-routing · ipv6 route ::/0</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-end"><div><Eyebrow label="CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING" /><h2>IPv6 Addressing</h2><p>Practice the key terms, procedures, and troubleshooting decisions from this module.</p></div><a href="/cisco/practice/">Start practice →</a></section>
</section>
